TI  - Predicting Crude Oil Futures Prices Using Multivariate and Univariate Models: A Comparative Study of LSTM, RF, ARMA, and Holt-Winters
AU  - Wu, Meng
AU  - Li, Zeyu
AU  - Wei, Yi
AU  - Chen, Ying
AU  - Gao, Mingyu
AU  - Huang, Dengke
AB  - This study integrates macroeconomic, commodity and political geography indicators to form a dataset used by algorithmic models to predict crude oil futures prices. Univariate models, including Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Random Forest (RF), ARMA and Holt-Winters, were evaluated for their ability to predict crude oil price volatility. The LSTM model outperformed the others in terms of accuracy and had a low margin of error, while the RF model captured the overall trend but performed poorly during periods of high volatility. The ARMA and Holt-Winters models show limited ability to predict long-term trends and short-term volatility. In multivariate forecasting, the inclusion of additional variables significantly improves the accuracy of the LSTM model, increasing its adaptability to complex price dynamics. In contrast, the RF model shows minimal improvement. In addition, the inclusion of geopolitical factors further improves the performance of the LSTM, demonstrating its adaptability to external market dynamics, while the RF model shows limited sensitivity to these factors, suggesting a reduced ability to deal with geopolitical influences. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s)
