TI  - Multihorizon forward forecasting of crude oil futures realized volatility based on dual-interpretable deep learning
AU  - Xing, Haiyang
AU  - Xu, Yejun
T2  - International Review of Financial Analysis
AB  - The precise measurement and forecast of crude oil futures volatility in financial markets is crucial for successful risk management and informed decision-making. Using five-minute high-frequency price data, this study constructs realized volatility (RV) for West Texas Intermediate Crude Oil (WTI) and North Sea Brent Crude Oil (Brent) futures and proposes a secondary decomposition-ensemble forecasting framework. This framework combines the secondary decomposition strategy comprising successive variational mode decomposition (SVMD) and robust empirical mode decomposition (REMD), with a hybrid deep learning model comprising a convolutional block attention module (CBAM), a bidirectional gated recurrent unit (BiGRU), and a temporal convolutional network (TCN), aimed at performing multihorizon forward forecasting of crude oil futures RV. Empirical analysis indicates that this framework generally outperforms other forecasting methods across multiple horizons, especially in short-term predictions, where it demonstrates high accuracy. Moreover, ablation experiments and Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) analysis provide a dual validation mechanism, enhancing the model's interpretability. The volatility-timing strategy further evinces its practical value in financial risk management. Overall, this study not only offers a more precise solution for forecasting crude oil futures volatility but also provides a solid theoretical foundation and practical tools for risk management and decision-making in financial markets. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.
