TI  - A Comparative Study of GARCH and Transformer Models for Volatility Forecasting: A Case Study on the iShares Global Clean Energy ETF
AU  - Lamsal, Yash Raj
AU  - Pandey, Madhav Prasad
AU  - Sapkota, Prabal
AU  - Jha, Shailendra Kumar
AB  - Volatility forecasting for clean energy assets is challenging because of their sensitivity to policy changes, technological innovation, and subsidy dependencies. Using the iShares Global Clean Energy ETF (ICLN), this paper compares forecasting performance between traditional Generalized Autoregressive Conditional Heteroskedasticity (GARCH) models and modern deep learning models like Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks and Transformer models. We use daily data from June 2008 to December 2025, and compute forward-looking realized volatility using a 21-day rolling window. We evaluate four GARCH-family specifications and find that GARCH (1,1) with normal distribution provides the best forecasting performance among econometric models. However, deep learning models consistently outperform traditional approaches, with a Transformer architecture achieving the lowest RMSPE of 0.2065 and RMSE of 0.0218 on the test set. The results shows that attention mechanisms in Transformers effectively capture the complex volatility patterns in clean energy markets with better forecasting accuracy. These findings have application in risk management, and portfolio optimization in renewable energy investments. © 2026 IEEE.
