TI  - Predicting WTI crude oil prices with a hybrid T-GARCH–BiGRU–KAN model: Integrating volatility modeling, deep learning, and interpretable neural networks
AU  - Zeng, Jin
AU  - Wu, Jingwen
T2  - Energy Sources, Part B: Economics, Planning and Policy
AB  - Accurate forecasting of crude oil prices is crucial for global economic stability, yet remains challenging due to their complex nonlinearity, volatility clustering, and structural breaks. This study proposes a novel hybrid T-GARCH–BiGRU–KAN model for forecasting daily WTI crude oil prices, integrating asymmetric volatility modeling, bidirectional deep learning, and interpretable neural networks. The T-GARCH component explicitly captures the leverage effect and filters conditional heteroskedasticity; the BiGRU layer learns complex temporal dependencies from both past and future contexts; and the KAN subnetwork provides fine-grained nonlinear refinement with interpretability potential. Using a comprehensive dataset from 1986 to 2024, the model is rigorously benchmarked against 16 alternatives, including standalone and hybrid variants. Empirical results demonstrate that the proposed model achieves superior forecasting accuracy across all metrics (RMSE, MAE, R², MAPE), with statistically significant improvements confirmed by Diebold–Mariano tests. It exhibits remarkable robustness across diverse market regimes, including crises such as the 2020 negative pricing event. This work contributes a potentially transparent, high-performance forecasting framework that bridges econometric rigor with a pathway toward interpretable deep learning, offering valuable insights for policymakers, investors, and researchers in energy economics and financial risk management. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
