TI  - Volatility prediction of high-frequency data based on deep feature extraction and fusion: An interpretable deep learning architecture
AU  - Liu, Shixiu
AU  - Zhu, Mengrui
AU  - Wang, Minggang
AU  - Tian, Lixin
T2  - Applied Soft Computing
AB  - Using a cross-domain technology integration strategy, a hybrid prediction architecture is developed for predicting high-frequency data volatility, with an emphasis on deep feature extraction and fusion. The architecture integrates front-end and back-end fusion. The front-end fusion breaks through the limitations of traditional single-category features, innovatively integrating econometric volatility prediction features with economic theory and network topology features reflecting market structure. Unlike fixed-weight fusion methods, the back-end design introduces a dynamic gating module that adjusts fusion weights according to the real-time importance of input features, improving adaptability to multi-source information. A dual-branch structure further enhances representation learning by unifying LSTM's global temporal modeling with TSMixer's local pattern extraction. Furthermore, the predictive outputs are linked with semi-parametric extreme value theory for risk measurement, and Shapley values are employed to assess feature contributions across forecast horizons. Focusing on high-frequency data from China's crude oil futures market, the target model demonstrates substantial improvements in volatility forecasting and risk measurement. Relative to GARCH, HMSE decreases by 80.935 %, 80.645 %, and 80.568 % for 1-, 2-, and 5-step forecasts, and asymmetric loss (ALF) for risk measures declines by 38.819 %, 40.042 %, and 39.143 %. Compared to a standalone LSTM, the reductions in volatility forecasting are 18.462 %, 5.785 %, and 31.937 %, while the reductions in risk measurement are 4.561 %, 0.668 %, and 8.977 %. Interpretation results highlight the primary role of econometric predictors and the important contribution of network topology features, particularly for 5-step-ahead forecasts. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.
