TY  - CONF
TI  - Crude Oil Price Forecasting Using Transformer and Recurrent Architectures for Improved Strategic and Operational Decisions
AU  - S, Nirmalganesh
AU  - Jidesh, P.
AB  - Forecasting crude oil prices over 60 day horizons is important for decision making in energy industry and commodity markets. Industries that rely heavily on crude oil such as refineries can benefit significantly from improved 30 to 60 day price forecasting, as it directly affects their procurement strategies, inventory planning, and risk management. There are many studies focusing on crude oil price prediction but forecasting at horizons over 30 to 60 days remains challenging. This work analyses various AI models such as XGBoost, GRU, LSTM, their bidirectional variants along with transformer architecture, for forecasting crude oil prices across multiple horizons. The models use more than 150 input features, including macroeconomic indicators, technical indicators used by traders, related commodity prices, and several engineered features derived from these variables. Forecast performance is evaluated for horizons of 1, 7, 14, 21, 30, 45, and 60 days. The models are trained using historical price data starting from 1990 and evaluated on an outof-sample test period from 2020 to 2025, which includes COVID-19 oil price shock and other market volatility. The results show that model performance changes across forecasting horizons. For short horizons XGBoost and GRU have performed well. Bidirectional models and Transformer remain stable and perform well at higher horizons. The Directional accuracy of Transformer based model is highest among the models, reaching above 70% at the 60 day horizon (H60), which indicates the usefulness of attention-based architectures for crude oil forecasting over a medium to long term horizon.
