TI  - Impact of COVID-19 on Crude Oil Price Prediction: A Comparative Performance Analysis of LSTM and ANN Models
AU  - Yannam, Adilakshmi
AU  - Matta, Nikitha
AU  - Kumar Dunga, Pradeep
AU  - Peddireddi, Srinivas
AU  - Vamsi Tadisetti, Leela
AU  - Naga Lakshmi Pandraju, Hari
AB  - Crude oil is a key component in the industrial production, and its price fluctuations often leads to economic instability. This research compares the performance of Artificial Neural Network (ANN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) models in predicting crude oil prices across pre-pandemic period (2010–2019) and post-pandemic (2020–2024) period. Analysis reveals that models performed strongly before COVID-19, with LSTM achieving higher accuracy (R-squared: 0.933) compared to ANN (R-squared: 0.912). However, during the pandemic period, both models showed decreased performance, with LSTM maintaining superior results (R-squared: 0.901) compared to ANN (R-squared: 0.865). Despite challenges in capturing extreme market volatility, both models demonstrate resilience, with LSTM consistently outperforming ANN due to its superior capability in handling temporal dependencies.
