TY  - CONF
TI  - Two-Phase Rolling Copper Price Forecasting for Min–Max Regret Procurement Decision and An Empirical Study for PCB Smart Production
AU  - Chen, Z.-X.
AU  - Chien, C.-F.
AB  - The printed circuit board (PCB) industry is a fundamental pillar of the world economy, supplying essential technologies to numerous industries, such as consumer electronics, artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure, and automotive. In general, copper-related raw materials account for approximately 30-40% of PCB manufacturing costs. However, the price of copper fluctuates due to demand-supply mismatches as well as international economic and political factors. This study develops a two-phase data-driven decision framework to predict weekly copper prices and support procurement decision-making. In the first phase, a Bi-LSTM-CNN-CBAM model generates the initial weekly price forecast, while a lightweight multilayer perceptron (Light MLP) performs residual correction to enhance forecasting accuracy. In the second phase, forecast residuals are converted into 81 price scenarios, derived from three possible price outcomes across a four-week decision horizon. A Min-Max Regret (MMR) criterion considers each possible price outcome generated from the forecasting scenarios to derive procurement strategies that minimize the maximum regret relative to ex-post optimal decisions. The proposed framework is validated through an empirical study in a world-leading PCB company. This study demonstrates that the two-phase framework provides a robust procurement decision to reduce supply chain risk under price volatility, thereby empowering digital transformation.
