TI  - Integrating Sentiment Analysis & Historical Data for Predicting Crude Oil Prices
AU  - Shameera, M. P. M. A.
AU  - Kesavan, S.
AU  - Arachchi, S. P. Kasthuri
AB  - Crude oil price forecasting remains a persistent challenge due to the volatility introduced by fast-moving geopolitical events and macroeconomic shocks. Currently available models mostly rely solely on time-series patterns or sentiment analysis. But these approaches do not capture the full complexity of short-term market dynamics. This research addresses this gap. Most times inaccurate forecasts can impact traders, risk managers, and policymakers globally. This study aims to develop a forecasting model that integrates domainspecific news sentiment with traditional market fundamentals to predict the next week’s crude oil price. We compiled a dataset of 60,000 English oil-related news articles, cleaned and timestamped them, and derived weekly sentiment features using VADER, FinBERT, and a domain-adapted CrudeBERT. These were combined with market variables including production, inventories, imports/exports, benchmark WTI prices, the Weekly Economic Index, and Geopolitical Risk Indices. A range of models, including linear regressions, tree-based methods, neural networks, and SARIMAX, were evaluated, alongside an ensemble that fused the best-performing tree-based and neural approaches. Best results were given by the ensemble (Random Forest + XGBoost + LSTM) with CrudeBERT based sentiment, which achieves the best accuracy: MAE ≈ 1.19 USD, RMSE ≈ 1.48 USD, MAPE ≈ 1.57%, and R2 ≈ 0.935. These results substantially outperform models that exclude sentiment or rely on generic sentiment features. The findings shows that combining domain specific news sentiment with economic and market fundamentals significantly enhances short-term crude oil price prediction. The novelty of this work lies in its unified framework that combines large-scale sentiment analysis from news articles with traditional oil market variables and geopolitical risk indices providing new evidence that such integration delivers materially improved forecasting accuracy.
