TY  - CONF
TI  - AI and Big Data for Optimizing Indonesia’s Oil Trade Balance
AU  - Ivana, Jane
AU  - Aleitta, Kyla
AU  - Setiyo, Khaylanissa
AU  - Perwiro Atmojo, Robertus Nugroho
AB  - Indonesia's oil trade balance, which was in surplus in the late 1990s, has gradually moved into an extensive deficit. Crude oil exports fell drastically throughout time from USD 5.71 billion in 1996 to just USD 2.23 billion in 2024. Exports of crude oil have fluctuated although they are still less than imports. With a peak of USD 31.76 billion in 2012, imports of oil products increased from USD 2.08 billion in 1996 to USD 25.92 billion in 2024. This study forecasts trade values and identifies abnormalities using artificial intelligence (AI) and big data. Grid-searched ARIMA models and an automated data pipeline were used with the best models, ARIMA (0,2,1) for exports and ARIMA (2,2,1) for imports, showing short term corrections after sharp changes. Events like the 2008 oil price rise and COVID-19 decline in 2020 are the main causes of volatility, according to Z-score results of year-over-year analysis. This suggests that persistent instability is not the root source of volatility. The model's capacity to offer a more realistic long-term prognosis is demonstrated by the final projection, which predicts a major decrease in oil exports and a large trade imbalance by 2030 when compared to the Hybrid VAR-LSTM approach model. The results suggest opportunities to boost export recovery and reduce reliance on imports. Data-based routing and AI forecasting are applied for this. To increase energy security, recommendations include incorporating more detailed data such as including outside variables in forecasts as well as AI-assisted distribution and refining. To improve forecast accuracy even more, future studies might expand this framework by adding advanced deep learning architectures like Transformer models or bidirectional LSTMs.
