AU  - Ashour, Marwan Abdul Hameed
AU  - Juma, Ahlam Ahmed
AB  - This study explores the effectiveness of traditional and artificial intelligence (AI)-based time series forecasting methods in predicting key economic indicators, specifically inflation rates and consumer prices, in the Arab world. Given the economic volatility in the region-driven by fluctuating oil prices, global market dynamics, and geopolitical uncertainties-accurate inflation forecasting is essential for informed and responsive policy decisions. Traditional models such as ARIMA are widely used for capturing linear patterns and seasonal trends, yet they fall short in modeling the nonlinear structures inherent in complex economic data. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a hybrid forecasting model that combines the strengths of ARIMA for modeling linear components with the adaptive capabilities of a Nonlinear Autoregressive (NAR) neural network to capture nonlinear dependencies. The hybrid ARIMA-NAR model was evaluated against the standalone ARIMA model using Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) and Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE) as performance metrics. Results indicate that the hybrid model achieved significantly lower forecast errors, with a 47.2 % improvement in RMSE and a 28 % improvement in MAPE compared to the traditional ARIMA model. These findings underscore the potential of AI-driven approaches in enhancing the accuracy of economic forecasts. Forecasts for inflation rates from 2024 to 2030 suggest a gradual decline, potentially signaling increased economic stability and the positive impact of ongoing policy reforms. The study contributes to the growing body of literature advocating for the integration of AI in economic modeling and offers valuable insights for policymakers and regulators in shaping evidence-based strategies to support sustainable economic growth across the Arab region.
