TI  - Exploring the Impact of Input Features on GAN-Based Stock Prediction
AU  - Gao, Yufeng
AU  - Feng, Jingfan
AU  - Tao, Zheng
AB  - This essay examines the impact of input attributes and training dataset size on stock prediction with Generative Adversarial Networks (GAN). The results indicate that more attributes do not necessarily lead to better model performance, and 2–3 sets of data attributes are often sufficient for good performance. The best set of data attributes includes a combination of core price information, news score information, and economic activities information, together with the combination of core prices information, news information, and mathematical information generated through Fourier transformation. The impact of different attributes on prediction performance varies depending on the size of the training set, with news information playing a positive role in smaller training sets and attributes such as stock index, mathematical information, and economic activities of the company having a positive impact on larger training sets. The future work involves validating these theories on different models and investigating the potential positive effects of standard information on larger training samples. Overall, this research highlights the importance of considering both the input attributes and training dataset size when using GAN models for stock prediction.
