AU  - Kumaran, Jayandra Sai
AU  - Sapkota, Aditya
AU  - Ranganathan, Prakash
AU  - Salehfar, Hossein
AB  - Accurate forecasting of Liquefied Natural Gas (LNG) is essential for global energy market stability, infrastructure planning, and policy development. Traditional static models often struggle to capture the complex, nonlinear, and multivariate nature of modern LNG data. This paper provides a literature review on the application of Artificial Intelligence (AI) models to improve the forecasting and market optimization of LNG. This review evaluates methodologies from supervised models such as LSTM and GRU, to hybrid architectures like CNN-LSTM to enhance precise forecasting and adaptability. Key performance metrics such as MAE, RMSE, MAPE, and R2 are used to evaluate the efficiency of these models. The review addresses the importance of preprocessing tools like Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and correlation analysis for dimensionality reduction and feature relevance. Critical factors affecting time-series forecasting, such as weather patterns, geopolitical events, and storage infrastructure, are analyzed. Finally, the review discusses the policy impacts and current challenges. It outlines future directions, including the integration of edge AI and regulatory feedback systems to improve the robustness and LNG frameworks with enhanced transparency.
