TI  - Machine Learning Techniques for Renewable Energy Forecasting in Thailand
AU  - Kochsuwan, Krist
AU  - Nonsiri, Sarayut
AU  - Kanignant, Tanyaporn
AB  - The effects of global warming have led to increasing environmental issues caused by the release of greenhouse gases, primarily from the use of fossil fuels, which remain the main energy source for humans. The volatility of crude oil and natural gas prices is influenced by global economic conditions, currency exchange rates, geopolitical issues, climate, and the fact Thailand's fossil fuel production capacity is insufficient to meet domestic demand. Therefore, transitioning from fossil fuels to renewable and alternative energy source becomes increasingly important. This study applied machine learning models, including Extremely Randomized Trees (ERT), Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGB), Light Gradient Boosting (LGB), Support Vector Regression (SVR), Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN), and Prophet to forecast total renewable electricity production (TREEP), total electricity consumption (TEC) and Proportion of TREEP and TEC (PTT) using meteorological data, economic indices, and population data. The goal is to predict the PTT value in Thailand over a 12-month period for decision-making by government agencies involve in the development and support of renewable energy. The results are shown Prophet suitable for TREEP, TEC and the PTT with MAPE of 2.709 %, 4.098 %, and 3.236 %, respectively.
