AU  - Rashid, Alidu
AB  - Global petroleum fuel pricing, increasingly governed by deregulated market regimes, exhibits significant volatility driven by crude oil prices, exchange rates, and shifting supply-demand conditions. These dynamics require advanced models capable of capturing both spatial interdependencies and temporal evolution across global markets. This paper proposes a mathematical framework for spatio-temporal forecasting and control using hybrid multi-agent graph-based reinforcement learning. Global fuel markets are modeled as graph-structured systems, enabling the representation of interactions between geographically distributed nodes alongside their time-dependent behavior. Multi-agent reinforcement learning introduces adaptive decision-making, supporting both forecasting and control under dynamic conditions. To demonstrate the framework, a Graph Attention Network with Gated Recurrent Units (GAT-GRU) is implemented. The model effectively integrates spatial attention and temporal sequence learning, achieving improved predictive performance. The results validate the framework's capability, offering a scalable foundation for analyzing and forecasting complex global energy markets.
