TI  - Empirical Analysis of AI Methods of Forecasting financial Times Series: A Research on LSTM and ML models in Indian Stock, Commodity and Energy Prices
AU  - Kumar, Suresh
AU  - Kumar, Pardeep
AU  - Sidhu, Renu
AU  - Kumari, Neha
AU  - Kaur, Swapandeep
AU  - Singh, Kawal Nain
AB  - The present paper provides an empirical analysis of the methods of Artificial Intelligence (AI) predicting the financial time series data in the Indian context, namely the use of Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) neural network, Random Forest Regression, and Support Vector Machines (SVM). There are four main variables of the study that are the NIFTY 50 stock index, the historical gold price, the price of crude oil and the price of natural gas which represent important sectors of the Indian economy. The paper demonstrates that the AI models outperform the conventional econometric models (such as ARIMA and Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression), significantly in terms of accuracy of predictions, sensitivity to volatility, and the ability to fit non-linearity in the actions of variables. LSTM reduced the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) by more than 50 percent in stock market forecasts when compared to ARIMA and the machine learning model improved the prediction performance of credit default by 17 percent compared to the logistic regression model. This research uses a mixed methods design, an Integrative survey, blending model-based performance evaluation with the qualitative contributions of 100 stakeholders in the Indian financial institutions, fintech affiliates, and regulatory authority. These findings reveal a wide optimism in terms of AI to enhance risk management and make the operations more efficient and also highlight the consistent concerns about data protection, clean governance and institutional readiness. The paper concludes by calling on the urgency of explainable AI systems (XAI), effective regulatory strategies, and industry synergies so that ethically and sustainably AI should be applied in the financial sector of forecasting, particularly in the emerging economies, such as India.
