TI  - A Hybrid Computational Intelligence Framework for PTA Futures Price Forecasting with Ridge Residual LSTM-Forest
AU  - Peng, Jiayi
AU  - Ma, Yanna
AU  - Wei, Wei
AU  - Wang, Hongxia
AU  - Hu, Mengyao
AU  - Shao, Yupeng
AU  - Shao, Bilu
AU  - Junfang, Fang
AB  - Purified terephthalic acid (PTA) futures forecasting is a challenging computational intelligence problem because prices are jointly driven by upstream crude oil and para-xylene costs, downstream polyester demand, spot-futures basis, market sentiment, and trading behavior. The resulting series contains linear autocorrelation, nonlinear residual patterns, feature redundancy, and regime-dependent volatility. This paper proposes a hybrid computational intelligence framework for next-day PTA futures price forecasting. The framework combines a Ridge linear anchor with a random-forest-screened LSTM ensemble, referred to as LSTM-Forest. Ridge regression first extracts the dominant linear component from all engineered features, while the LSTM-Forest module learns the remaining nonlinear residual from selected features and rolling historical windows. A double Bollinger-band module is further used to evaluate the decision-support value of the predicted price curve. Using daily PTA futures data from December 2006 to April 2026, with training data up to 2024, validation data in 2025, and testing data from January to April 2026, the selected configuration achieves a test MAE of 76.08 CNY per ton, RMSE of 107.07 CNY per ton, MAPE of 1.23 percent, directional accuracy of 67.11 percent, and correlation of 0.9889. Benchmark and ablation experiments show that linear models are strong competitors, random-forest feature screening improves the LSTM-Forest module, and derivative loss does not provide stable gains under the current setting. The results indicate that residual learning is an interpretable way to integrate linear structure, neural ensemble learning, and trading-signal analysis for commodity futures forecasting.
