TI  - Hybrid Meta-Ensemble Learning for High-Frequency Price Forecasting in the Australian National Electricity Market
AU  - Panagopoulos, Michael
AU  - Zhou, Zeyang
AU  - Storey, Thomas
AU  - Thiyagarajan, Karthick
AU  - Moreira, Catarina
AU  - Prasad, Mukesh
AB  - Short-term electricity price forecasting in Australia’s National Electricity Market is challenging due to five minute settlement intervals, extreme volatility, and increasing renewable energy penetration. This study aims to enhance operational forecasting accuracy through a sensing-driven metaensemble that remains robust across regions with varying market dynamics. The proposed TriModal MetaBoost integrates Long Short-Term Memory networks, XGBoost, and Random Forest via a meta-regressor. Engineered features capture temporal structure, momentum, volatility, and stress signals. The model is trained and evaluated on nine years of data covering five regions, and is benchmarked against eleven baselines and four hybrid competitors using RMSE, MAE, R<sup>2</sup>, and sMAPE. MetaBoost achieves an average RMSE of 3.068, MAE of 0.970, R<sup>2</sup> of 0.9992, and sMAPE of 2.574%, outperforming alternatives in New South Wales, Queensland, and Victoria. In contrast, PSO-XGBoost performs better in South Australia and Tasmania. Treating market signals as sensed data streams and combining heterogeneous learners yields state-of-the-art accuracy in the NEM’s five-minute environment. The evidence supports region-aware ensemble design and motivates adaptive weighting strategies for real-time bidding, risk management, and system operations.
