TI  - Aviation Refueling Strategies Based on Neural Networks and Optimization Algorithms
AU  - Peng, Hao
AU  - Zhou, Jingfeng
AU  - Li, Zhihong
AU  - Ksibi, Albaraa
AU  - Xu, Xiaoying
AU  - Zhang, Qinxue
AB  - Aviation fuel costs account for 30% 40% of total airline operating expenses. Therefore, optimizing refueling strategies to reduce fuel consumption and costs is crucial. Traditional fuel consumption optimization methods often struggle with complex flight networks and poor adaptability to dynamic environments. This study proposes an intelligent aviation fuel optimization system based on neural networks and optimization algorithms, combining deep neural networks, polynomial regression, and gradient optimization algorithms to improve the accuracy of fuel consumption prediction and optimize refueling strategies. The system builds a fuel consumption prediction model using historical data and meteorological information, and applies polynomial regression to reduce computational complexity. The optimization process, based on gradient descent methods, refines refueling plans while meeting safety constraints. The optimization objective can be configured to minimize either fuel costs or consumption. The system supports dynamic fuel price input and adjusts strategies based on real-time fuel prices and supply conditions. It enhances prediction accuracy and flexibility, automatically adjusts to price fluctuations, and improves operational efficiency and economic benefits for airlines.
