TI  - A Proposed Framework Using a Hybrid Structural-XGBoost Model for Brazilian Electricity Spot Price Forecasting Considering Hydrological Regimes
AU  - Evangelista, Clarissa Do Nascimento
AU  - Oliveira, Gabriel Almeida Santos De
AU  - Souza, Lawany Da Silva
AU  - Sales, Diego Câmara
AU  - Duarte, Victor Beltrão Valente
AU  - Silva, Luiz Eduardo Sales e
AB  - The Brazilian short-term electricity price, known as the Difference Settlement Price (PLD), exhibits high volatility driven by hydrological conditions, demand shocks, and operational constraints. Most existing forecasting studies rely heavily on hydrological variables-such as Affluent Natural Energy (NEA) and reservoir level, which limits their applicability when such data are unavailable or delayed. To address this, this paper investigates whether raw climatic variables alone can effectively explain PLD dynamics. We propose a two-stage hybrid framework combining Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression with eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) to capture both linear trends and complex, non-linear market relationships. Evaluating the model’s robustness under water stress, the framework achieves superior predictive stability with a Weighted MAPE of 13.99%, significantly outperforming standalone machine learning approaches. The architecture intrinsically captures climate interactions, naturally decoding the impact of thermal shocks during drought regimes without relying on artificial penalization or risking overfitting. Ultimately, by mapping extreme values and mitigating financial risk during price spikes, this approach proves that integrating a structural baseline outshines pure deep learning benchmarks.
