TI  - An Applicability Study on Traditional Machine Learning and Large Language Models for Time-series Data Trend
AU  - Pan, Ruoqi
AU  - Li, Manjia
AU  - Huang, Ke
AB  - The futures time series feature is nonstationarity and varying volatility across different commodity types. This work formulates futures trend forecasting as a three-class classification problem and sets a \pm 0.0005 fluctuation threshold at the minute-level dataset. It compares CatBoost, MultiRocket and TimeGPT on the data for gold, corn, and crude oil. The temporal input matrix and sliding window are configured for MultiRocket and TimeGPT, respectively. Experiments indicate that TimeGPT achieves the highest overall accuracy of 0.8033 with balanced classification performance. CatBoost achieves high accuracy on partial datasets but shows clear category bias, whereas MultiRocket performs the worst. This study provides quantitative evidence for model selection between traditional machine learning and large time-series models in multicategory futures prediction.
