TI  - Computational Economics: Modeling Energy Markets with AI
AU  - Sahu, Manish RK
AU  - Tune, Hadasha Nobel
AU  - Goyal, Eeshita
AU  - Kulkarni, Shailesh
AU  - Amonov, Mehriddin
AU  - Fazliddin, Tuyev
AB  - The markets of energy are becoming complicated as the demand changes, integration of renewable sources, geopolitics and price volatility poses a challenge and the traditional econometric models could not help in making accurate predictions and design of the policy. This paper deals with the issue of low flexibility and inefficiency in predicting the energy market models. The main goal is to create an AI-assisted computational economics framework that will provide an opportunity to capture nonlinear interactions and dynamic market behaviour. The suggested solution involves a combination of machine learning and economic modeling in order to increase the accuracy of the forecast and efficiency of decision-making. In particular, the hybrid models that integrate Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Random Forest, and Reinforcement Learning are used to predict the past price patterns, demand-supply patterns, and the influence of the policy. It has been experimentally proven that the proposed AI-based structure is more effective in terms of predicting prices by 27.6 percent, forecasting errors by 34.2 percent, and the efficiency of demand estimation by 22.8 percent than the traditional econometric models. Also, the accuracy of policy simulation improved by 31.4 points, which allowed the improvement of strategic planning. The findings support the fact that AI in combination with computational economics make the models much more robust and responsive.
