TY  - CONF
TI  - Dynamic Pricing-Driven Framework for Optimizing Energy Consumption
AU  - Vieira, Douglas A. G.
AU  - e Horta, Gustavo de L.
AU  - Silva, Gustavo R. L.
AU  - Tavares, Leonardo D.
AB  - This article presents a framework for calculating the hourly electricity price, considering the relationship between energy consumption and energy price in such a way to achieve the consumption closer to a given target. This framework considers a general definition between energy consumption and price, the objective function as a distance metric from the desired target, and a set of constraints. Two optimization methods were tested, the Trust-Region Constrained method and Sequential-Least Squares Programming (SLSQP). The results showed that it is possible to model the consumption/price relationship dynamically, and that this scenario was designed to encourage consumption outside of peak hours, not being restricted to the two forms of modeling presented. This aspect is particularly useful for short and long-term financial planning for energy suppliers. This flexibility in pricing strategies is a significant advancement in understanding consumer behavior in response to price changes. The paper opens avenues for further exploration, such as investigating alternative linear constraints, different price limits, and varying time intervals for consumption. This suggests a pathway for enhancing the framework’s applicability and effectiveness in real-world scenarios.
