TI  - Hybrid LSTM-CNN Model for Forecasting Electricity Prices: A Comparative Study with Standalone CNN and LSTM Models
AU  - Gamel, Samah A.
AU  - Sultan, Yara A.
AB  - The electricity price forecasting is important in energy markets because it allows the energy market stakeholder to optimize the consumption level, minimize the cost, and the stability of the grid. This paper discusses the use of deep learning, such as Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs), Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks, and a Hybrid CNN-LSTM model, in electricity price prediction. The data set include time-series electricity price data and extraneous variables like the load of the system, temperature and CO2 intensity. The models are evaluated based on Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), Mean Absolute Error (MAE) and Mean Absolute Percentage error (MAPE). Reports on our findings have revealed that CNN shows the lowest error values, but the Hybrid CNN-LSTM model has a balanced trade-off since it uses both spatial and temporal information resulting into more stable and predictive forecasts over time. The paper notes the usefulness of the hybrid deep learning structures when it comes to financial time-series prediction and proposes future research recommendations.
